        narrative: "Chilika Lake in Odisha is the largest coastal lagoon in India and the second largest in the world. It is a brackish water lagoon separated from the Bay of Bengal by a long sandy spit. The salinity of the lake fluctuates dramatically based on the season: during the monsoon, freshwater rivers dump silt and water into the basin, while in winter, seawater enters through the narrow inlet. This dynamic hydrology supports a rich fishery and is the primary habitat for the endangered Irrawaddy dolphin and a crucial wintering ground for migratory birds.",
