        narrative: "Jog Falls, located in the Western Ghats of Karnataka, is one of the highest plunge waterfalls in India. Created by the Sharavati River dropping 253 meters in a single leap, the falls consist of four distinct cascades: Raja, Rani, Roarer, and Rocket. The falls are highly seasonal: during the dry months, they shrink to thin streams, but during the monsoon, the water volume swells into a massive, roaring wall of white water. The river's hydrology is heavily managed for hydroelectric power generation through the Linganamakki Dam.",
